    "Native destructor events record original slot addresses and actual callback-time Size/Capacity/Data publication. Rust events encode the corresponding original metadata snapshot without creating receiver aliases, while token metadata, original pointer and tracked hole range are directly checked before/after failure. This does not claim callback-time receiver-alias support.",
    "The uninstrumented public token executable matches all thirty-five native, failed-repair and restored-source observations, without accessing test recovery hooks or cleaning native leaked replacement allocations.",
    "The preceding ordinary safe reserve baseline destroys all old values and publishes the replacement after first/middle/final Drop panic. A new integration regression proves that existing sound cleanup policy remains unchanged. Native destructor behavior is an explicit unsafe token adapter, not a silent mutation of safe reserve.",
    "Strict Miri runs the full native matrix with explicit replacement cleanup. It also verifies repeated repair failure, owned panic-payload pointer identity, shared relocation-failure ownership, zero-sized and borrowed/auto-trait domains, and interrupted-token Drop retaining genuine raw provenance until separate typed test reconstruction.",
